{
  "requirement": "Under {cite}`requirements.cli.skill_structure`, the `habeas-refactor` skill MUST be the home of moving code, tests, and requirements to another file, chapter, or outline node, and of renaming requirements.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "structural: The only structural clause in `requirements.cli.skill_structure.refactor` is that the skill named `habeas-refactor` is the home of moving code, tests and requirements, and of renaming requirements. The supplied root is the whole skill file, `assets/skills/habeas-refactor/SKILL.md`. Its frontmatter has `name: habeas-refactor`. Its description covers moving code, tests or requirements to another file, chapter or outline node, and moving or renaming a requirement. It also sends changes to what code or requirements say to `habeas-requirements`. The body has a `Habeas refactor` section with a `Workflow` section and a `Gotchas` section. The intro paragraph explains how moves change addresses and how `habeas refactor` rewrites them. The workflow mentions `habeas info` for finding selectors. I could not see most of the workflow and gotchas text, because the reads returned it elided. The delegation to `habeas-requirements` and the way the description is written do not suggest the skill assigns the topic elsewhere.\n\nThe parent `requirements.cli.skill_structure` adds that a skill is its `SKILL.md` plus the files under `references/`, and that each topic is stated in full in exactly one skill. This is a rule about where content lives, not about how code is built. No library, signature, type or delegation through a named interface is prescribed. Where a topic lives is the observable content of the skill, so there is no other structural obligation to map to implementation. The root supports the one clause that does exist.\n\nbehavioral: The requirement has two obligations: `habeas-refactor` covers moving code, tests, and requirements to another file, chapter, or outline node, and it covers renaming requirements. It must also be the home for those topics, so no other skill states them in full.\n\n`tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_refactor_skill_is_home_of_moves_and_requirement_renames]` covers both obligations:\n- **Moves:** `assert_text` runs on the `## Workflow` section of `habeas-refactor`'s `SKILL.md`, which `section` extracts. It asserts the phrases for moving a `{req}` directive to a new chapter, for a file or directory mapping (`--map src/config.rs=src/settings.rs`), and for a node mapping (`--map`). These cover moves to another file, chapter, or node.\n- **Renames:** it asserts the definition mapping (`--map requirements.cli.check=requirements.core.check`). It also asserts the phrase \"Renaming a requirement's ID is a definition mapping\" anywhere in the skill, using `assert_phrases`.\n- **Home, exactly one skill:** `assert_home(\"habeas-refactor\")` asserts that the refactor skill contains every marker from `home_markers`. Those markers include the rename mapping, how references are rewritten, `habeas refactor --dry-run`, the node mapping form, and the \"Would leave ... unchanged\" note. It also asserts that no other bundled skill contains any of those markers. A skill that restated them would therefore fail the test.\n\nThe assertions are real `contains` checks that panic on failure, so an implementation missing the home text or duplicating it elsewhere would be caught. I found no unmet obligation or missing scenario.",
