        "rationale": "The only structural clause in `requirements.cli.skill_structure.refactor` is that the skill named `habeas-refactor` is the home of moving code, tests and requirements, and of renaming requirements. The supplied root is the whole skill file, `assets/skills/habeas-refactor/SKILL.md`. Its frontmatter has `name: habeas-refactor`. Its description covers moving code, tests or requirements to another file, chapter or outline node, and moving or renaming a requirement. It also sends changes to what code or requirements say to `habeas-requirements`. The body has a `Habeas refactor` section with a `Workflow` section and a `Gotchas` section. The intro paragraph explains how moves change addresses and how `habeas refactor` rewrites them. The workflow mentions `habeas info` for finding selectors. I could not see most of the workflow and gotchas text, because the reads returned it elided. The delegation to `habeas-requirements` and the way the description is written do not suggest the skill assigns the topic elsewhere.\n\nThe parent `requirements.cli.skill_structure` adds that a skill is its `SKILL.md` plus the files under `references/`, and that each topic is stated in full in exactly one skill. This is a rule about where content lives, not about how code is built. No library, signature, type or delegation through a named interface is prescribed. Where a topic lives is the observable content of the skill, so there is no other structural obligation to map to implementation. The root supports the one clause that does exist."
